a("ILO Generative AI and Jobs index", ent="ilo-genai-exposure-index",
  mt="THE ILO GENERATIVE AI EXPOSURE INDEX ON THE OVERVIEW — WORKING PAPER 140, 24%, 3.3%, 34% AGAINST 11%, TRANSFORMATION OVER REPLACEMENT, RIGHT; A GENDER FIGURE (29% AGAINST 16%) NOT IN THE PAPER; THE UPPER-BOUND LIMIT ABSENT",
  d="THE ILO GENERATIVE AI AND JOBS INDEX, ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-29: 'The International Labour Organization (ILO) published its updated assessment in the report \"Generative AI and Jobs: A Refined Global Index of Occupational Exposure\" (ILO Working Paper 140).' Seven findings (24% of workers exposed; 3.3% in the highest gradient; transformation over replacement; 34% in high-income against 11% in low-income countries; women nearly twice as exposed as men, 'roughly 29% vs. 16%'; the most and least exposed occupations) and the method (task data points, four gradients). Eight cards, two of them the ILO's.",
  reading="The innocent reading first. The Overview names the paper and gives its headline figures and method, from the ILO's pages and commentary.",
  findings=["THE INDEX HELD. Working Paper 140, 24%, 3.3% in the highest gradient, 34% against 11% by income group and the transformation reading ('most jobs are more likely to be transformed than made redundant') are the record's. " + CK,
            "A GENDER FIGURE NOT IN THE PAPER. The paper states that women are more affected than men; its own figures for the highest gradient are 4.7% of female and 2.4% of male employment worldwide. 'roughly 29% vs. 16% across sampled countries' is in no saved source; the paper's 29.2% is the 2023 index's combined share of augmentation and 'the big unknown'.",
            "TWO COUNTS RUN TOGETHER. 'nearly 30,000 to over 52,000 individual task data points' joins the 29,753 Polish tasks scored and the 52,558 data points; the record keeps them as two measures.",
            "THE LIMITS ABSENT. The estimates measure exposure and give an upper threshold of potential, and the paper says they are no measure of actual employment effects; a static snapshot at the start of 2025; a survey run in Poland, comparable only within each occupational group. The composition's 'can be heavily impacted' stays inside exposure, and gives no limit."],
  analysis="An exposure index is composed at its headline grain, with one figure the paper does not hold and without the paper's own statement of what exposure is not. The record's negative tags (not an observed outcome; not a forecast) are what separate this resource from the employment trackers beside it in the census.",
  per_v={"author": False, "inst": True, "id": True, "src": True}, per_note="The ILO named; no author in the answer (the authors in a card); Working Paper 140 named; the ILO's own pages cited.")

